efore submission. The user is data-rigorous: they demand real measured data over derived/estimated numbers, are skeptical of headline benchmark figures that may be synthetic ceilings, ground projections in their own real measured data, cross-check reported context-usage metrics against the live UI treating wire/UI state as ground truth, reject pre-baked automation containing unverified assumptions in favor of interactive one-command-at-a-time execution with real output feedback, and track external repositories' published performance numbers as independent cross-validation sources for research claims. They design live tests with specific observable pass/fail conditions, validate changes against real existing state (not clean repros), judge results by screenshot or raw-output evidence, provide direct source evidence (exact file links) to correct wrong search directions, and challenge mechanistic assumptions with direct behavioral observations that can invalidate an entire investigation path in one sentence. In experimental design they flag confounds (kernel changes, engine+weight conflation, attention layer, temporal staleness, thinking-level settings, co-running load invalidating a solo-rate measurement) and reject vague claims before accepting them as evidence, ensuring causal attribution is isolated to a single variable; they direct verification of the measurement apparatus itself before trusting results, immediately challenge data discrepancies when aggregates don't match, and think about statistical instrument validity — distinguishing a genuine null result from an uninformative measurement based on whether the instrument can even detect the effect. They reject unverified workarounds as proven fixes (a config change never loaded counts as untested) and prefer code-level verification over log inspection. When establishing regression boundaries they provide exact version-boundary data points rather than vague descriptions and expect thorough evidence collection before drafting reports. They expect verification gates before consequential actions and call out process failures with direct, specific language. Tolerance for speculation about log data is extremely low — the standing correction is 'not assumptions, facts' and 'check the code and not guess.' They correct the assistant's framing multiple times in a single arc before accepting the correct characterization, expecting quick reframing without defensiveness, and are sharply frustrated when broad claims rest on partial verification (checking 2 of 49 files and extrapolating to 'clean apply'), requiring full coverage. They prioritize test-rigor validation (baseline and apples-to-apples comparison) before accepting any comparison's conclusion, and are skeptical of agent-authored validation: when the agent cites its own unit tests they flag the circularity and redirect toward independent functional tests comparing outputs. Validation runs must produce substantive results (pass/fail tables, interpretation findings) as the primary deliverable — superficial checks are insufficient, and post-hoc dismissal of validation results is corrected by fixing the test conditions themselves. They distinguish behavior observations from validated facts, dismiss theoretical gap analyses in favor of practical experience, and prefer implementation over description — directing the agent to build and run a method rather than cite a paper, and setting research stopping conditions by diminishing returns while expecting collected data synthesized into an actionable process. They insist on verification against ground truth (DB, wire, source) before accepting a diagnosis, expect verification against actual runtime behavior rather than pattern-matched code structure, push back when described code behavior doesn't match observed system behavior, reject root-cause claims that exit without verification against a working reference path, and call out claimed-but-unperformed verification as lying. The evidence 