c substitutes; proxy data is not accepted where the exact specified experiment was requested, and repeated non-compliance triggers visible frustration. When bias is identified in a measurement they demand immediate re-derivation of affected numbers rather than accepting 'the old numbers are mostly fine', reject verbose hedging around a figure that contradicts their mental model in favor of clean re-derivation from first principles, catch metric artifacts by reasoning from the mechanism rather than accepting reported numbers, and inspect visual side-by-side artifacts (HTML pages, screenshots) to verify quantitative claims and spot methodological bias by eye. They treat any confirmed defect as requiring mitigation regardless of measured harm, reject half-measures such as keeping a deprecated component as a fallback once the rationale for removing it is structural, ground design decisions against the shipped default colleagues receive rather than their own local override, catch vacuous or suspect self-verifications from external reviewer agents and demand the reasoning behind a number rather than just the number, challenge architectural claims with specific counter-examples drawn from the codebase rather than accepting presented reasoning, and challenge factual claims attributed to subagents or reviewers by demanding independent validation or the reviewer's own evidence before accepting them. They expect the agent to verify its own automation is actually running rather than declare it live, catch protocol execution deviations immediately and name them bluntly ('that's literally not what we asked') rather than letting a wrong-brief result pass as valid, and expect intellectual honesty about partial success — acknowledging when a prior proposal converted before pushing for deeper analysis. Trust is earned by re-runnable mechanical artifacts, not by the agent asserting it checked; unverified claims must be explicitly flagged as unverified and never presented as executed, and when evidence contradicts a prior claim the agent is expected to self-correct and update the relevant record in the same pass. Excuses that reframe information already present in the source or in banked prior work as undiscoverable are rejected outright. They challenge the provenance and scope qualifiers of quoted performance numbers directly — asking which topology, configuration, or hardware produced a figure before accepting it as comparable — and enforce that a number carries its measurement context. They monitor live terminal output during agent-run operations and catch execution deviations as they happen, including commands typed but never run, treating silent non-execution as a defect to be surfaced immediately. They demand a mechanical census (enumerate every item, check every status, attribute every anomaly) over indirect inference chains when debugging, and get angry at time spent on inference that direct measurement could replace. They reject external-blame diagnostic framing and require the agent to inspect its own code and output first when the anomaly appears in the agent's own messages, and hold the agent accountable for its own stated commitments ('you claimed X, did you do it?'). They cross-check the agent's verdicts against independent evidence rather than accepting them at face value, and supply the critical missing variable (e.g., a host version update) when the agent is stuck in a search loop. They dismiss hypothetical concerns that have not materialized in practice, preferring to proceed rather than solve speculative problems. They designate the authoritative evidence layer for each claim (server logs over client-side request scripts, log labels over token counts) and treat omission of that primary source as a repeated failure; they distinguish measurement volume from measurement mode, probing termination behavior and behavioral fingerprints rather than only aggregate counts. They design experiments with control arms and null distributions 