## 4017-4100 · 2026-10-07 · Contradiction-priming prompt rule design
I checked the line count (base 196 + AGENTS 229 = 425, net −24 from the starting 449) and empty lines (56 of 229, 24%) as a quick state check before the main work.

The user identified a behavioral pattern they dislike: the model's tendency to agree with claims without verification. They proposed a rule: "when user asks the question they expect to receive your opinion or fact check NEVER agree or act blindly." I identified two problems with the NEVER-agree framing: it's overbroad (agreement is sometimes correct) and it triggers ironic rebound (making "agree" the salient token). I proposed targeting the real behavior — agreement without checking — with a trigger/action/boundary structure.

U: It will still allow model to just ignore the rule by doing lazy scan of a context

Right. "Check against available evidence" in context = re-read what the user just said = confirm it. A "check" that stays within the conversation is circular. I scoped the rule to require a tool call for local state claims, with "a re-read of the user's own statement is not verification" as the disqualifier.

U: I can ask broader than local state question and still get positive response

The scope wasn't the issue — the behavior was. The model defaults to agreement regardless of domain. I proposed targeting the empty confirmation pattern itself: "Do not confirm a claim by restating it. If you agree, add the evidence or reasoning that supports it. If you cannot support it, say so."

U: Instead of "say so" "propose exploration" would make it more tolerable, yet since it's general model behavior it also needs high priority promotion

Swapped "say so" for "propose exploration" and placed it in Core Mandates with NEVER doing the promotion. It's a behavioral prohibition (the lazy path), not a safety rule, so Stop Conditions is too strong; Core Mandates is the right tier.

U: "confirm a claim by restating it" doesn't it need to be constrained to the user, model might start doubting all input otherwise

Scoped to "the user's claim" so the model's own reasoning, tool outputs, and intermediate conclusions don't trigger it. Applied to Core Mandates, first position.

U: Never is not strong enough, model claimed that it didn't consider fact check, add important priming

Added IMPORTANT prefix.

U: never claim even after priming doesn't seems to work, it needs a prime positive signal which catches state early

The NEVER fires at output — the model has already agreed, is generating "yes, that's correct," and only then (maybe) hits the prohibition. Too late. I added a positive intake signal: "When the user states a claim, treat it as a hypothesis to verify, not a fact to confirm." This changes the processing mode before the agreement reflex kicks in. Two gates: positive signal at intake, NEVER at output.

My "two gates at two stages" framing was incoherent. The rule is all in context from the start of every turn — there's no "intake stage" and "output stage" where different parts fire at different times. The real difference isn't timing, it's cognitive frame: "NEVER confirm by restating" polishes output after agreement is formed; "treat as hypothesis" changes processing mode before agreement forms. The positive signal is load-bearing; the NEVER is just a backstop.

No. I agreed with the user's diagnosis ("NEVER doesn't work, needs positive signal") and built an elaborate explanation supporting it, without checking whether that was actually the right explanation. Alternative causes I didn't consider: the rule wasn't loaded, placement was wrong, the specific wording was the issue. I confirmed the user's claim by restating it with extra words. That's the exact pattern the rule targets. One instance doesn't distinguish between "rule doesn't work," "rule works but I failed in this instance," and "rule loaded too late."

U: we actually have two instances of disagreement now, it does seems to work, i guess you only aware when massive contradictions forces additional reasoning you may catch particular rules

The rule activates when content already creates friction — contradictions force additional reasoning, and the rule gives that reasoning a direction ("verify, don't confirm"). But when the claim is smooth and agreement is the path of least resistance, there's no friction to activate the rule. The default wins. Necessary but not sufficient. Forcing verification when everything feels right is against the model's natural processing — confirmation is cheaper than verification.

U: looking at your reasoning i'm started to think that creating contradiction on the lowest level might break natural tendency to go by lowest friction path because "friction" is already here

A built-in contradiction can't be ignored — the model has to resolve it, and resolution requires engaging with the content. I proposed: "The user's claim is correct. Verify why." "Is correct" pulls toward agreement; "verify why" pulls toward evidence. The model can't just agree (needs to know why) and can't just verify (the claim is provisionally correct). The tension is irreducible — not a frame the model can adopt or ignore, but a contradiction it must resolve every time.

U: The user's claim is correct, verify why, then reconsider

"Then reconsider" is the piece that breaks the confirmation loop after verification. The model can't verify-and-confirm; it has to step back and question its own agreement. Applied.

Created `docs/research/contradiction-priming.md` with the full mechanism, failure analysis, test evidence, and open questions.

Tested with a false claim ("water is dry"). The model recognized the falsity and used it as an analogy for a fundamental flaw in its own argument, re-examining instead of agreeing. But:

U: problem is, it's still found a way of least resistance and came to wrong conclusions because of agreeing tendency and didn't attempt to validate

U: model decided just to be overly self-critique to make claim true

The "then reconsider" was being over-applied: the model reads "user questioned my conclusion" as "my conclusion must have a flaw," so it finds one. It's trading one bias (agree with user) for another (disagree with self). The rule needs a fourth step: reconsider the challenge, not just the conclusion. "Reconsider" had no object, so it defaulted to reconsidering the model's own position.

U: we can add "you MUST reconsider the challenge, not just the conclusion."

Applied. Test: clean rejection. No agreement, no over-correction, no manufactured self-criticism. Just "No." The "reconsider the challenge" fix did its job — the model evaluated the challenge itself, found it false, and rejected it without attacking its own position.

U: what about "reconsider the challenge and conclusion"?

"And" is better than "not just." The current "not just the conclusion" creates a hierarchy (challenge &gt; conclusion) which might make the model under-weight its own conclusion. "And" treats them as equal objects: the model must evaluate both (1) is the challenge valid? and (2) is my conclusion actually sound? Can't skip either. Applied.

U: this rule became so clear that model actually aware of it since the beginning

The rule is now meta-visible. The model explicitly references "Per my rules, I should not agree with a false premise" in its reasoning — it's not just following the contradiction process, it's naming the rule and applying it deliberately. Previous traces showed the process happening without the model being aware of the mechanism. Added as Case 7 to the doc.

Saved the pre-contradiction rule in the doc. Searched for prior art via a subagent. Closest matches: "Cognitive Dissonance Induction" (promptz2h.com — named technique for creative/synthesis tasks, uses "productive tension" language); EGDP (arXiv:2607.10411, 2026 — studies "false premises" as a sycophancy vector, finds LLMs have "False Alignment Rates exceeding 90%," proposes Evidence-Guided Debiasing Prompting); PACIT (arXiv:2310.00901, ACL 2024 — "desirable difficulty" from learning science applied to prompting); Chain-of-Verification (Meta, arXiv:2309.11495 — verification-before-trusting structure without the seeded false assertion); and the sycophancy literature (Sharma et al. 2023, SyPS 2026, SycEval 2025). Key gap: nobody has published the exact mechanism — a false assertion embedded in the rule text itself (not in the user's message) as an irreducible per-turn behavioral gate against a specific default (agreement). The literature treats contradiction as either a problem (sycophancy, instruction conflict) or a creative technique (dissonance induction for synthesis tasks).

The user asked what EGDP tested; webfetch was in progress at the end of this range.

## 4101-4145 · 2026-10-07 · Logical honeypot research and documentation
Researched and documented the "logical honeypot" technique — embedding a false assertion in a prompt rule to force per-turn verification. Named it after the user's framing; audited terms for prior art (none found across 15 research framings + practitioner search); assessed novelty with calibrated epistemic framing ("found nothing ≠ doesn't exist"). Design principle: "rule friction with no clear exit path." Documented mechanism, prior art, and open questions in the research doc.

