I removed the `tools:false` entry from `opencode.jsonc`, re-enabling `task` fully (execution allowed, agent-type list returns). Final state: `task` exists as a working inline tool but all prompt routing points at `paseo_create_agent` — available for rare one-offs, not advertised as the delegation path.

User then asked about the `dreamer*`/`historian*`/`smart-note-compiler` denies visible in the probe output. I verified those are Magic Context's own per-name task denies (isolating its internal agents from being spawned as subagents by ordinary agents).

User asked whether a plugin could hide the tool definition. I checked the installed `@opencode-ai/plugin/dist/index.d.ts:316`: the `tool.definition` hook receives `(input: { toolID: string }, output: { description: string; parameters: any })` — one tool's definition body, mutated in place. No tool map to delete from, no return contract, no registration control. Plugin ceiling: neuter text, replace executor, never unlist. True removal is an upstream capability gap.

