## 4825-4894 · Designed task-as-paseo-plugin override (argument translator to create_agent)
The user asked whether the rewriting plugin (overriding opencode's built-in `task` tool) would preserve the dynamic subagent list. I explained that the list is opencode-internal (built from `describeTask` filtering `mode: subagent`), and a same-name override replaces the whole `ToolDefinition` — so out of the box you'd get a dead hand-written description. Two options: build-time list (bake at plugin init from `client.agent.list()`) or per-request refresh via `tool.definition` hook.

U: "1. is fine since subagents aren't loaded without restart anyways"

Build-time is exact, not approximate — agents only load at server start. Spec solidified: `tool: { task: {...} }` override, description = original task wording + agent list, args = `{description, prompt, subagent_type}`, execute validates subagent_type against the list.

User asked to brainstorm further. I surfaced the structural hazard: blocking wait + Paseo's permission flow can deadlock (child parked on pending permission never reaches terminal state; parent sits in `execute()` forever). Proposed relay (poll pending permissions, bridge to parent's `context.ask`), stall watchdog (120s no activity → partial return), abort propagation, no wall-clock cap.

U: "well i guess it would be reasonable to not override anything since agent can decide for himself whether aproove or not"

This cascaded: if the plugin mediates nothing, `execute()` cannot block (a blocked parent can't call `respond_to_permission`). The two decisions are welded — no mediation ⇒ return-fast. Tool becomes: spawn, return immediately with agent id + poll/respond instructions.

U: "can't override just spawn create_agent instead of playing a relay?"

This collapsed the entire relay architecture into an argument translator. `execute()` maps `task(description, prompt, subagent_type)` → `create_agent(title, initialPrompt, settings:{modeId}, provider:&lt;inherited&gt;)` invoked over the daemon's own `/mcp/agents` endpoint. Everything Paseo's `create_agent` does comes free: subagent link, notifyOnFinish, labels, workspace defaulting. Reimplement nothing — just remove the composition cost.

U: "well, go on if it's going to work the way you say it will just announce itself exactly like task tool but on call will just spawn create_agent and session will get native paseo subagent with all it's features"

Acceptance contract stated. I verified the mechanism from source: Paseo injects its MCP as a remote HTTP config entry (`mcpServers.paseo = {type: "http", url: "&lt;daemon&gt;/mcp/agents?callerAgentId=&lt;agentId&gt;", headers: {Authorization: "Bearer &lt;token&gt;"}}`). Token is per-boot `randomUUID()`, caller identity is a query param. The daemon URL + token are in `OPENCODE_CONFIG_CONTENT` env var (set at process spawn by Paseo). Wrote design doc to `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-29-task-as-paseo-plugin-design.md`.

U: "make sure that we don't have hardcoded port because this mcp most definitely tooks a random one"

Patched doc: port is ephemeral, `OPENCODE_CONFIG_CONTENT` is the only source, parsed fresh at each plugin init, no default-port guessing.

U: "it would be better if it's just log error and stay inert instead of breaking anything"

Then the critical correction:

U: "i meant inert is logging error and not overriding task tool at all because model will just go into 'fine i'll do it myself'"

This defined the fail-inert policy: no Paseo at init → log once, omit `tool.task` from the hooks export entirely — built-in `task` survives untouched. Registration decides at startup whether to override at all. No stub, no "I'll do it myself" detour.

User then asked about timing races (wait window for Paseo resolving). I showed the ordering is deterministic: env is set at process spawn, token minted before server spawn, daemon-side agent records precede any model turn. Only residual race is session/reconnect-time caller binding — covered by call-time retry-once. User confirmed the path.

Spike started (4888-4894): attempted to verify daemon endpoint empirically. Hit issues — the port I found (52250) was from env but the route 404'd, suggesting it's not the daemon HTTP port. Was enumerating Paseo's listeners when the chunk ends.

## 4895-4950 · Validated task-as-paseo-plugin spawn mechanism
Completed the spike battery for the task-as-paseo-plugin design (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-29-task-as-paseo-plugin-design.md). Three spikes resolved the spawn mechanism:

**S1** (identity resolution): caller identity comes from scanning `~/.paseo/agents/&lt;ws&gt;/&lt;id&gt;.json` on disk — no daemon round-trip needed.

**S2** (mode flip): `create_agent(settings.modeId="explore")` is refused server-side with `Invalid mode 'explore' ... Available modes: build, plan` — the daemon validates modeId against the same UI-filtered list produced by `isSelectableOpenCodeAgent` (opencode-agent.js:471,3705-3716). The two-step workaround (`create(armed in plan)` → `set_agent_mode(type)` → `send_agent_prompt`) was verified: the flipped child ran with explore's actual toolset (write absent), self-reported and reverted its own workaround, left no file behind.

**S2b** (stacking test): the user's concern — "if you spawned with plan it might actually also not have rm" — demanded a discriminating test. Armed in `plan`, flipped to `coder`, asked to actually write. Result: `write` genuinely works ("Wrote file successfully" in the timeline). The flip replaces the definition; no plan residue. Arming in plan is safe for writer types.

U: well, that's why i've asked about feeding because having them in ui is kinda bad

The user rejected the `mode: all` config approach (flipping subagent definitions from `mode: subagent` to `mode: all` in `~/.config/opencode/opencode.jsonc` would make them spawnable directly but also puts them in Paseo's UI mode picker). The crux: Paseo's `isSelectableOpenCodeAgent` predicate serves dual duty — it feeds both the UI mode list AND the creation validator — so no opencode-side config shape splits "spawnable at creation" from "visible in UI." Three paths forward were laid out: (1) keep the two-step (current verified design, zero patch surface), (2) local patch of Paseo daemon's `create-agent-mode.js` to check against the full `client.app.agents()` list instead of the selectable subset, (3) upstream ask separating the two concerns. The chunk ends with the user asking "maybe plugin can do it?" — the agent is about to investigate whether the Paseo plugin surface offers a creation-time config transformation hook that could split the predicate without patching the daemon.

Memory #2356 was corrected to the measured facts (create_agent validates against the filtered list; the two-step is the surviving public path). Probe agents archived. Design doc updated to match reality.

## 4951-4999 · Closed task-as-paseo-plugin design: verified hook-only spawn, ruled out one-plugin collapse
I verified the final capability questions for the task-as-paseo-plugin architecture and closed the design.

The HOOKTEST probe (agent e09f7692) confirmed the hook-injected `modeId: "explore"` governs the first turn's toolset on a hook-created child — `write` absent, explore's toolset present, no substitution performed. The direct path works: opencode-side plugin makes one `agents.create()` call with a marker in the title, Paseo-side `agent.create.before` hook rewrites `modeId` from the marker.

U: "what market? paseo-side expected just to make it possible to schedule subagent class agents"

I explained "marker" is the channel carrying `subagent_type` past the validator (a title prefix or label), then investigated whether the app/SDK RPC path could accept `modeId` directly — the user's expectation that Paseo-side should "just make it possible." I wrote a WS probe against the daemon's `/ws` endpoint (raw port URL never completes handshake — must use `/ws` path per websocket-server.js:487). Result: the app/SDK path runs the **same validator** as MCP `create_agent`. Both doors reject subagent `modeId` with `Invalid mode 'explore'`. The hook is the only mechanism in; the marker is how intent reaches it past the validator.

Bonus from the probe: the WS SDK path (`createPaseoClient({url})` + `agents.create({config: {provider}, parent, prompt, title})`) is superior to MCP-HTTP for the opencode side — one call with `parent` binding and `prompt` built in, no JSON-RPC hand-rolling. WS URL discovery noted as implementation item (env `baseUrl` is the wrong listener at 52250; daemon `listen` at 6767 is correct).

