Honest gap — my smokes verified spawn shape (mode, workspace, parent link) then archived the child before checking whether it reported back. The notification path I verified earlier (probe e09f7692) came through `paseo_create_agent` MCP with `notifyOnFinish`, not through `spawnDelegate`. The SDK `create` options I saw had no `notifyOnFinish` field. I started probing whether SDK create carries notification semantics — chunk ends mid-investigation.

## 5095-5119 · Proved MCP-create loop end-to-end and investigated tool-override surface
Continued the task-as-paseo-plugin work from the prior chunk. The SDK exposes `agents.subscribe` (client-side stream) but no finish-subscription RPC — registration lives inside `createAgentCommand` behind the MCP-kind gate. So the full loop requires the MCP channel for the create call itself (where `notifyOnFinish` + auto parent-labeling live), with the daemon-list identity resolution kept.

Probed the MCP route: first hit 404 on the env listener (port 52250), then found the MCP route is built from the same `listen` target as the WS — port 6767 (the daemon port). `agent-mcp` v2.0.0 live on the daemon port with the env token. Ran a full-loop test with a unique token: child `75c1874a` created via MCP, workspace pinned, hook applied `explore` on the MCP path too. The child finished and the notification arrived back at the parent with `PLING-4821-VERIFIED` — the full loop (MCP create → child runs → finish notification to parent) is proven end-to-end.

Then edited paseo-task.ts multiple times to wire the MCP-create path into the plugin; 32/32 tests green, tsc clean.

U: well, google says that opencode supports MCP to override it's default tools

Investigated this claim against both docs pages. The override capability is real but belongs to *custom tools*, not MCP. `custom-tools.mdx`: "Custom tools are keyed by tool name. If a custom tool uses the same name as a built-in tool, the custom tool takes precedence" — with a literal example replacing built-in `bash`. That's `~/.config/opencode/tools/task.ts`, opencode-side files. MCP cannot override a built-in — the MCP doc's own note: "MCP server tools are registered with server name as prefix" (`mymcpservername_*`). Paseo's tools arrive as `paseo_*`, CodeGraph's as `codegraph_codegraph_explore`. An MCP server physically cannot present the bare name `task`. The "full paseo path" (Paseo plugin injects an MCP server carrying the implementation) gives a *competing* `paseo_task` next to the surviving built-in — two `task` tools, routing by prompt hope. Not the replacement.

What the finding does open: a simpler opencode-side surface than the plugin — the documented custom-tool file instead of `Hooks.tool`. Two unverified deltas before swapping: whether the precedence covers `task`'s dynamic description the way it covers `bash`, and whether custom-tool `context` carries `abort` (docs show `sessionID`/`directory`/`worktree`, no abort). Recommendation: finish the plugin path first (built, unit-tested, restart acceptance next); evaluate the custom-tool file as a candidate simplification only if plugin registration disappoints live.

Also closed the notification gap from the earlier question: SDK-created children are silent (`create.js` gates finish-subscriptions to `kind === "mcp"`), so `spawnDelegate` now spawns via MCP `create_agent` — the PLING-4821 notification was that path proven end-to-end.

