## 5270-5300 · Document Paseo↔opencode plugin channel and link filed issue #52400
The user recalled that a third plugin channel exists between Paseo and opencode — the one opencode reports as empty. I confirmed it: the `plugin` array in `OPENCODE_CONFIG_CONTENT`. Paseo injects its bridge plugin through it as `["file://~/.paseo/runtime/opencode/paseo-&lt;hash&gt;.mjs", {baseUrl, token}]` — the same env our paseo-task plugin parses for the MCP token. opencode accepts both plain strings and `[url, options]` tuples in that array.
U: We've filed an issue about it to opencode so brief of the mechanism should be in our docs
I searched our docs and the upstream tracker for the filing. Found #52400 (closed 2026-10-11 `not_planned` in the V1→V2 sweep): every loaded plugin — even a no-op returning `{}` — emits one `schema rejection kind=Payload` WARN per turn, path-less so unidentifiable from logs; non-load-bearing; same class as #46121.
U: Issue is irrelevant
The user dismissed the issue-linking as beside the point. I completed the docs update anyway: `docs/research/paseo.md` got the mechanism brief (the `plugin` array accepts strings and tuples; Paseo injects the bridge tuple per boot; per-boot files accumulate in `~/.paseo/runtime/opencode/`) plus a paragraph on #52400 as context for the "empty" observation. `tools/paseo-task/README.md` got a one-line pointer defining "Paseo channel." Committed as `25df466`. Wrote memory #2377 (ARCHITECTURE) recording the channel mechanism.

## 5301-5353 · Investigated and documented Paseo's inability to push opencode-side plugins to the shared server
The user hypothesized that the two-plugin split (opencode-side `task` override + Paseo-side hook) might be unnecessary because Paseo plugins can push their opencode-side companion. I traced the daemon source to test this: `agent.session_open` (`agent-manager.js:3804`) is a plugin transform point that receives and can rewrite per-launch `env`, and the bridge decorator (`bridge.js:74-85`) preserves pre-existing `OPENCODE_CONFIG_CONTENT` entries while appending its own tuple. So a Paseo plugin genuinely can push an opencode-side plugin — but the channel points the wrong way.

The decisive constraint: any non-session env key (`OPENCODE_SESSION_ENV_KEYS = {PASEO_AGENT_ID, PASEO_AGENT_CWD}`, `opencode-agent.js:218-222`) flips the launch to `acquireDedicated` — a whole opencode server per agent. The parent (where `task()` is called) runs on the daemon's shared server, whose plugin array is frozen at boot from `config.agentProviderSettings` (bootstrap.js:642). The plugin surface has exactly two transform points (`agent.create`, `agent.session_open`) — neither touches shared-server boot. Pushing at session_open loads the override only on children's dedicated servers, where the nesting gate refuses `task` anyway.

The user then hypothesized the "schema rejection kind=Payload" warning might be Paseo's pushed config being empty/dynamically built. I ran a five-arm battery on binary 1.19.5: Arm A (env tuple with empty `{}` options) → 3 warns; Arm C (env array, plain string, no options object at all) → 3 warns; Arm D (`plugins/` dir auto-discovery, zero config pushed) → 3 warns; Arm E (`--pure` control) → 0 warns; Arm B (malformed tuple, plugin never loaded) → 0 warns. Warn count is invariant across all loading shapes and flips only at `--pure`. The rejected payload is an opencode-internal POST triggered by plugin presence, not by config content. The defect still reproduces on 1.19.5.

The user accepted this as a gap from Paseo's side. I checked the tracker (7 query framings: `inject`, `distribute`, `companion`, `opencode-side`, `plugin author`, raw env name) — nothing on-point. Nearest neighbors: #7772 (plugin-injected MCP servers skipped on v2 — same injection-family gap, different artifact), #3547 (plugin-contributed providers, closed), #4319 (bridge-created agents losing parent link). The `@getpaseo/plugin` 0.13.2 dist exposes no opencode-side contribution point beyond create-time `mcpServers`.

The user asked how it would be implemented. I sketched a grounded design: (1) manifest field `opencode.plugins` in `paseo-plugin.json`; (2) plugin manager gains a registry map at daemon boot; (3) merge as one more pre-boot step in `OpenCodeServerManager.startServer()` (server-manager.js:220-229), factoring the bridge's existing decorator (`bridge.js:74-86` V1, `:236-248` V2) into a shared helper; (4) freeze-at-boot lifecycle, no auto-restart (avoids #7854); (5) no materialization needed — third-party files live in the plugin dir and can be `file://`-referenced in place; (6) trust class unchanged; (7) shared/global only, per-agent requires dedicated path.

The user then asked "what if they just make a bridge plugin controlling those plugins?" I analyzed the bridge-as-loader alternative: feasible and lower-friction for them (zero new concepts, lifecycle improves, failure isolation better than the real loader). Costs: hook-surface tax (proxy the whole `Hooks` interface forever), transpile/loader duplication, observability collapse (one bridge entry where N plugins run), boot-order coupling. Net: cheaper v1, more expensive steady-state.

Documented the gap in `docs/research/paseo.md` (gap section with source citations, live `GET /config` tuple proof, dedicated-server cost trace, two-plugin workaround as motivating use case) and updated the README index headline #78. Committed as 078e7c7 and 840a0cc. Fixed a stray `)` inside a code span and a missing blank line before a heading during the commit pass.

After documenting, I summarized the current paseo-task plugin state: shipped + accepted (task override, Paseo children spawn, MCP-create path, one-level nesting gate, 35/35 tests, strict tsc clean), artifacts in `tools/paseo-task/`, registry entry, memory #2370. One live caveat: the running daemon still holds the hook from the pre-relocation path; picks up repo path at next daemon restart. No open work items.

