## 5402-5410 · 2026-10-29 · Investigated Paseo's subagent-mode gate and mode:all workaround
U: Let's test it out, ask exploration subagent why paseo gated opencode hidden subagents so we could use them without paseo-side plugin

Dispatched an explore subagent to research why Paseo excludes `mode: "subagent"` opencode agents from selectable agents. The child returned findings (truncated at section (c), pulled full via get_agent_activity). I verified all load-bearing claims against primary sources.

**Why the gate exists** — inheritance, not a decision:
- Commit `6f34699e` (2026-03-15, "Add OpenCode build and plan modes") born the mode picker as `mode === "primary" &amp;&amp; !hidden`. The provider's original commit (2026-01-11) had no filter.
- PR #606 / commit `3dae11d2` (thisisryanswift, merged 2026-05-02) extracted `isSelectableOpenCodeAgent` and widened to `primary | all`; subagent exclusion rode along unchanged, pinned by test at `opencode-agent.test.ts:1514`.
- Stated reasons: issue #605 boundary ("top-level picker = top-level agents; hidden internals stay hidden") and `docs/agent-lifecycle.md:186` — provider subagents are child executions owned by the provider, deliberately not inserted into AgentManager as managed agents.
- Nobody ever argued "subagents must be hidden because X" — the split is opencode's own semantics encoded as a default, never revisited.

**Could we have used them without the Paseo-side plugin?** Yes technically — `"agent": { "explore": { "mode": "all" } }` passes `isSelectableOpenCodeAgent`, spawnable via `create_agent` and task-invokable inside opencode. But `hidden` only applies to `mode: subagent`, so a `mode: all` agent lands in Paseo's UI mode picker — exactly the vetoed "having them in ui is kinda bad" ruling. No flag/env bypasses the predicate Paseo-side. The hook-rewrites-modeId path remains the only hidden-but-spawnable route.

The finding adds an upstream angle: #605/#606 show they widen the picker when asked — the missing ask is "spawnable without selectable," same dual-duty split already in the plugin-contribution gap section.

## 5411-5439 · Assessed Paseo 0.14 release impact on our plugins
The user noted Paseo 0.14 released with the headline "Plugins can now add tools to individual agents and configure their MCP servers when sessions open." I checked the installed daemon (still 0.13.2) and pulled the two relevant PRs (#7938 defineAgentTool, #7983 launch-only mcpServers at agent.open.before) from their shipped docs.

Key findings: `defineAgentTool` executes callbacks in the plugin process (only metadata + validated inputs cross); attachment is strictly additive; name collisions with Paseo tools or other plugins on the same agent **fail the session open** — confirming our one-plugin-collapse ruling structurally. The `agent.session_open` hook was renamed to `agent.open.before` with new `tools`/`mcpServers` fields. Plugin reload makes open sessions stale → idle reopen before next prompt (#5427, the gap our shared-browser attach-backfill patch works around). Idle agent runtimes release after one hour and resume on next prompt (#7782).

U: will our plugin work in it, their version check is kida wonky and disables plugins just because version mismatch while api didn't change whatsoever

I verified three independent reasons our plugin survives: (1) our manifest uses `&gt;=0.13.0` (bare minimum, no upper bound) — the wonky-disable risk hits caret/bounded ranges like `^0.13.0`; (2) our hook `server.before("agent.create", ...)` still exists in 0.14's `PluginLifecycleRegistration.before()` and `PluginBeforeRequests` still carries `"agent.create"`; (3) the pinned `@getpaseo/plugin: 0.13.2` devDep is type-only (erased at build), zero runtime coupling. Caveat: surface-level verification (types + docs + manifest semantics), not a live run — the daemon is 0.13.2. One behavioral neighbor to watch: the create-mode validator (`create-agent-mode.ts`) — #7938 touched its test file but not the validator itself, so our modeId-rejection workaround should be unchanged.

