This corrected my diagnosis. Descriptions ARE consulted and fire 100% when keyed on **conversation events** (a topic the user's request contains — "drawing a mermaid diagram"). They fail at zero when keyed on **behavioral phases** ("before proposing fixes", "about to claim work is complete") because self-monitoring the model's own workflow phase is the measured-weak capability. The fix is re-keying descriptions from behavioral-phase triggers to conversation-event triggers, keeping the decision model-side but moving it onto the capability surface that demonstrably works.

I drafted all 12 rewrites (brainstorming, writing-plans, executing-plans, subagent-driven-development, test-driven-development, systematic-debugging, requesting-code-review, receiving-code-review, verification-before-completion, finishing-a-development-branch, using-git-worktrees, dispatching-parallel-agents). The duty edge ("before proposing fixes" etc.) survives as the right side of each description; only the left-side trigger changes from self-monitoring to request-surface matching.

